{
  "evidence": [
  {
  "id": 1,
  "fact": "On June 23, 2016, the U.S. Supreme Court issued a 4-4 per curiam opinion in United States v. Texas, affirming the Fifth Circuit's injunction against DAPA, which left the program blocked and not being implemented.",
  "source_date": "2016-06-23"
  },
  {
  "id": 2,
  "fact": "The Supreme Court's 4-4 tie in United States v. Texas did not decide the merits of DAPA's legality and created no precedent, but it kept the nationwide injunction in place.",
  "source_date": "2016-06-23"
  },
  {
  "id": 3,
  "fact": "Judge Andrew S. Hanen issued a preliminary injunction on June 22, 2015, blocking the implementation of DAPA, which was upheld by the Fifth Circuit and subsequently the Supreme Court.",
  "source_date": "2015-06-22"
  },
  {
  "id": 4,
  "fact": "The Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals affirmed the preliminary injunction against DAPA on November 9, 2015, maintaining the block on the program.",
  "source_date": "2015-11-09"
  },
  {
  "id": 5,
  "fact": "As of the knowledge cutoff date of January 20, 2017, there was no public record of the injunction being lifted or the Supreme Court reversing its position to allow DAPA implementation.",
  "source_date": "2017-01-20"
  },
  {
  "id": 6,
  "fact": "The inauguration day for the next presidential term following the 2016 election was scheduled for January 20, 21, 2017.",
  "source_date": "2016-12-19"
  }
  ],
  "reasoning": "Based on the evidence, the DAPA program was blocked by a preliminary injunction issued by Judge Hanen on June 22, 2015 [3], which was affirmed by the Fifth Circuit on November 9, 2015 [4]. The U.S. Supreme Court subsequently issued a 4-4 tie decision on June 23, 2016, which left the Fifth Circuit's injunction in place and prevented DAPA from being implemented [1][2]. As of the knowledge cutoff date of January 20, 2017, there was no indication that the injunction had been lifted or that the program was set to be implemented prior to the inauguration day [5]. The inauguration day was scheduled for January 20, 2017 [6]. Therefore, based on the status of the legal proceedings and the lack of any reversal of the injunction, it is highly unlikely that DAPA will be implemented prior to inauguration day.
  },
  "probability_yes": 0.0,
  "prediction": "no"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "As of 2015, there were no official announcements by any state of withdrawing from the Schengen Agreement, only temporary reintroductions of internal border controls.",
      "source_date": "2015-02-12"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "No sovereign country had left the Schengen Area before 2017, with the area characterized by enlargements and temporary border checks rather than withdrawals.",
      "source_date": "2017-01-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "In 2016, 22 of the 28 EU member states were part of the Schengen Area, and no member state that had joined had subsequently withdrawn.",
      "source_date": "2016-12-31"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "The UK did not withdraw from Schengen in 2015 as it had never participated in the agreement and maintained an opt-out.",
      "source_date": "2015-09-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "The Schengen Agreement has no formal withdrawal mechanism, and historically, countries have only temporarily suspended border-free rules during crises.",
      "source_date": "2016-06-15"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "Based on the historical record available before 2018, no state had ever officially announced a withdrawal from the Schengen Agreement. Evidence shows that while countries like France, Germany, and others temporarily reintroduced internal border controls in 2015 and 2016 due to the refugee crisis, these were suspensions of the border-free rules, not withdrawals from the agreement itself [1][2][3]. The UK, which had a referendum on EU membership in 2016, had never been a member of Schengen and thus could not withdraw [4]. Furthermore, the Schengen framework was designed to allow for temporary measures rather than permanent exits, and no legal precedent for withdrawal existed prior to 2018 [5]. Therefore, there is no basis to predict an official announcement of withdrawal before 2018.",
  "probability_yes": 0.0,
  "prediction": "no"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "The FBI and DHS released a Strategic Intelligence Assessment on Domestic Terrorism in May 2021, which noted that 2019 was the deadliest year for domestic violent extremist acts since 1995 with 32 fatalities, and that the FBI was conducting about 1,000 domestic terrorism investigations annually.",
      "source_date": "2021-05-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "The Department of Homeland Security issued a bulletin warning of threats of domestic terrorism following the storming of the Capitol Building in January 2021.",
      "source_date": "2021-01-07"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "As of the pre-2022 data available, the only U.S. mass shooting in 2021 with 10 or more fatalities was the Boulder, Colorado shooting on March 22, 2021, which resulted in 10 fatalities.",
      "source_date": "2021-05-26"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "The Atlanta spa attacks in March 2021 resulted in 8 fatalities, which is below the 10-fatality threshold.",
      "source_date": "2021-03-24"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "The San Jose shooting in May 2021 resulted in 9 fatalities including the gunman, which is below the 10-fatality threshold excluding the perpetrator.",
      "source_date": "2021-03-24"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "No other 2021 U.S. incident with 10 or more fatalities was documented in pre-2022 sources as an act of domestic terrorism.",
      "source_date": "2021-12-31"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "The question asks if an act of domestic terrorism resulting in 10 or more fatalities will occur before January 1, 2022. Based on the evidence, the only incident in 2021 meeting the 10-fatality threshold was the Boulder, Colorado shooting on March 22, 2021, which is already in the past relative to the cutoff date [3]. The Atlanta and San Jose incidents fell below the 10-fatality threshold [4][5]. While the FBI and DHS reported high numbers of investigations and a history of deadly acts, no new incident with 10+ fatalities occurred after the Boulder shooting in 2021 according to pre-2022 data [1][6]. Therefore, no such act occurred in the remaining months of 2021 before the specified deadline.",
  "probability_yes": 0.0,
  "prediction": "no"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "In May 2021, Hamas accused Israel of violating the Gaza ceasefire framework by continuing actions in Jerusalem, specifically regarding evictions in Sheikh Jarrah and clashes at Al-Aqsa Mosque, and stated that Israel must stop these actions as a condition for calm.",
      "source_date": "2021-05-21"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "Hamas official Izzat al-Reshiq stated after the ceasefire that Israel had to cease its transgressions in Jerusalem and address the destruction in Gaza, warning that Hamas still had its 'hands on the trigger'.",
      "source_date": "2021-05-21"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "On June 15, 2021, the IDF stated it had targeted Hamas military facilities in response to incendiary balloons launched from Gaza into Israel, describing these launches as a violation of the ceasefire.",
      "source_date": "2021-06-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "On June 16, 2021, the IDF reported striking Hamas military compounds in response to incendiary balloons launched from Gaza.",
      "source_date": "2021-06-16"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "On June 17, 2021, Al Jazeera reported that Israeli media and the military said they targeted Hamas positions as a reaction to the ongoing launch of incendiary balloons and vowed to hold the group accountable.",
      "source_date": "2021-06-17"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "UN reporting from June 2021 described the ceasefire as 'precarious' and noted retaliatory Israeli strikes following incendiary balloons and other tensions.",
      "source_date": "2021-06-17"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "The evidence confirms that both sides made accusations of ceasefire violations before July 22, 2021. Hamas accused Israel of violating the ceasefire in May 2021 by continuing actions in Jerusalem, specifically regarding Sheikh Jarrah and Al-Aqsa, and by not meeting stated conditions for calm [1][2]. Subsequently, the Israeli government and IDF accused Hamas of violating the ceasefire in June 2021 by launching incendiary balloons from Gaza, leading to retaliatory strikes on Hamas military facilities [3][4][5]. These events directly satisfy the condition of either party accusing the other of violating the agreement.",
  "probability_yes": 1.0,
  "prediction": "yes"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "The deadliest direct military confrontation between the U.S. and Iran in 2020 was the U.S. drone strike that killed Iranian General Qasem Soleimani on 3 January 2020.",
      "source_date": "2020-01-03"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "On 8 January 2020, Iran launched ballistic missiles at two Iraqi military installations hosting U.S. forces, including Al Asad Airbase and a base in Erbil.",
      "source_date": "2020-01-08"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "By 15 March 2021, publicly available reporting described the January 2020 episode as the most severe U.S.-Iran confrontation in decades, but there had been no repeat of direct lethal strikes between the two states in the same period.",
      "source_date": "2021-03-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "On 15 April 2020, 11 Iranian IRGC Navy vessels made dangerous, close-range approaches to U.S. Navy and Coast Guard ships in the northern Persian Gulf, but no fatalities were reported in this incident.",
      "source_date": "2020-04-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "On 10 May 2020, an Iranian missile strike on the support vessel Konarak during naval exercises killed 19 Iranian sailors, but this was a friendly-fire accident, not a confrontation with U.S. forces.",
      "source_date": "2020-05-10"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "In late 2020 and early 2021, maritime threats including a mine on a tanker and a tanker seizure occurred, but no fatalities were reported in those incidents.",
      "source_date": "2021-02-28"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "The historical record prior to the cutoff date shows that the last lethal direct military confrontation between U.S. and Iranian national military forces occurred in January 2020, involving the killing of Qasem Soleimani and the subsequent missile attack on U.S. bases [1][2]. Between January 2020 and the cutoff date of 15 March 2021, multiple incidents occurred, including dangerous naval maneuvers in April 2020 and maritime seizures, but none resulted in fatalities for either side's military forces [4][6]. The only fatalities in the region during this period were from a friendly-fire accident involving Iranian forces in May 2020, which did not involve U.S. military personnel [5]. Public reporting as of the cutoff date explicitly states that there had been no repeat of direct lethal strikes between the two states since the January 2020 exchange [3]. Therefore, based on the established pattern of non-lethal escalation and the explicit absence of new lethal events in the reporting, it is highly probable that no such event occurred before the cutoff.",
  "probability_yes": 0.0,
  "prediction": "no"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "The AlphaGo vs. Lee Sedol match was a five-game series scheduled for March 9, 10, 12, 13, and 15, 2016, in Seoul, South Korea.",
